{"id":"u-3315725e-2c1c-4454-bd5a-2ca3961567a9","src":"community","title":"Golden Era Bodybuilding: Full Body 5-Day","author":"Mithosphere","url":"https://www.boostcamp.app/users/b1vu9i-golden-era-bodybuilding-full-body-5-day","desc":"PURPOSE\nThis is a 12-week muscle-building regimen designed to build classic size, symmetry, and proportion using free-weight basics. Built on the training styles of the 1970s, this program prioritizes high-quality repetitions, full range of motion, and steady progressive overload. By utilizing a high-frequency, full-body layout, it stimulates muscle groups multiple times per week to drive efficient, consistent hypertrophy.\n\nMETHODOLOGY\n- STRUCTURE: The training layout dictates five consecutive full-body training days executed from Monday through Friday, followed by two complete days of rest on the weekend.\n- SESSIONS: Each daily workout is anchored by a heavy, low-rep compound primary lift designed to build absolute mechanical tension and neurological drive. Following the heavy anchor, the session shifts to a secondary compound movement targeting an 8 to 12 hypertrophy rep range, and finishes with a sequence of high-effort accessory isolation exercises to sculpt structural balance.\n- FOCUS ROTATION: To safely sustain a five-day full-body frequency without overtraining the central nervous system, the daily emphasis rotates systematically. The workload shifts logically from heavy horizontal pressing and quad dominance, to posterior chain hinging and vertical pulling, to high-volume anterior leg work, to overhead pressing power, and finally to upper body width, arm mass, and structural detail.\n- FREQUENCY: Rather than obliterating a single muscle group once every seven days with excessive, junk-volume sets, this system distributes total weekly volume across three distinct touchpoints. This frequent re-stimulation keeps muscle protein synthesis continuously elevated without inflicting deep, irreparable muscle tissue damage during any single workout.\n- PROGRESSION: Keeping a meticulous training log is a mandatory requirement. Progressive overload is driven entirely by tracking and beating specific load, set, and repetition milestones on the exact same exercises week over week, rather than introducing random exercise variations that break data continuity.\n\nWARM-UP & RECOVERY\n- PREPARATION: 5-8 minutes of easy general work to warm-up. 2-4 warm-up sets for most of the exercises. On squat and hinge days add a set of light hip and ankle work before you load the bar. The accessories do not need their own warm-up, the first working set is the warm-up.\n- CARDIO: Keep hard cardio to two easy 20 to 30 minute sessions on the weekend if you want it.\n- FATIGUE: Any joint that hurts for more than two sessions in a row gets a movement swap, not a heavier warm-up. If your top sets stall on three consecutive days across two weeks, you are under-recovered, not undertrained.\n\nEXTRAS\n- ARM WORK: If needed, 1 extra set of curls or pushdowns on Day 2 and Day 5 is fine.\n- NO PULLUPS?: If you have a bar, swapping a pulldown for chin-ups or pull-ups is a straight upgrade. Do that.","level":["Intermediate"],"goals":["Muscle","Strength"],"equipment":["Garage Gym"],"minutes":70,"joins":0,"rate":0,"rateCount":0,"updated":"2026-10-23T21:42:18.488Z","variations":[{"name":"default","days":["Squat & Press","Hinge & Pull","Legs & Chest Volume","Overhead & Arms","Width & Detail"],"weeks":[[{"ex":[{"n":"Squat (Barbell)","sets":[{"lo":6,"hi":8,"pct":70,"x":4}],"note":"Rest 2 minutes between sets."},{"n":"Incline Bench Press (Barbell)","sets":[{"lo":6,"hi":8,"pct":70,"x":4}],"note":"Rest 2 minutes between sets."},{"n":"Bent Over Row (Barbell)","sets":[{"lo":6,"hi":8,"rpe":8,"x":4}],"note":"Rest 90 seconds between sets."},{"n":"Lateral Raise (Dumbbell)","sets":[{"lo":12,"hi":15,"rpe":8,"rpeHi":9,"x":3}],"note":"Rest 60 seconds between sets."},{"n":"Standing Calf Raise","sets":[{"lo":12,"hi":15,"rpe":8,"x":3}],"note":"2 seco